## My answer to the prompt

You can use Unreal’s native audio-capture API without an `AudioCaptureComponent`. One important caveat: in a networked game, `AGameMode` runs on the server, so local microphone capture should generally be started on the owning client (for example, in a PlayerController or LocalPlayer subsystem). The example below shows the capture lifecycle in a GameMode for a standalone/single-player case.

Enable the **Audio Capture** plugin and add the appropriate capture module for your UE version (commonly `AudioCaptureCore`) to your `.Build.cs`. UE’s exact API names can vary by version; the following illustrates the `Audio::FAudioCapture` stream API.

```cpp
// MyGameMode.h
#pragma once

#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "GameFramework/GameModeBase.h"
#include "AudioCaptureCore.h"
#include "MyGameMode.generated.h"

UCLASS()
class MYPROJECT_API AMyGameMode : public AGameModeBase
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    virtual void BeginPlay() override;
    virtual void EndPlay(const EEndPlayReason::Type EndPlayReason) override;

private:
    void StartMicCapture();
    void StopMicCapture();

    TUniquePtr<Audio::FAudioCapture> Capture;
};
```

```cpp
// MyGameMode.cpp
#include "MyGameMode.h"

void AMyGameMode::BeginPlay()
{
    Super::BeginPlay();
    StartMicCapture();
}

void AMyGameMode::StartMicCapture()
{
    Capture = MakeUnique<Audio::FAudioCapture>();

    Audio::FAudioCaptureDeviceParams Params;
    Params.DeviceIndex = INDEX_NONE;       // Default input device
    Params.SampleRate = 48000;
    Params.NumInputChannels = 1;

    const bool bOpened = Capture->OpenAudioCaptureStream(
        Params,
        [](const float* Audio, int32 NumFrames, int32 NumChannels,
           int32 SampleRate, double StreamTime, bool bOverflow)
        {
            // Called on the audio-capture thread. Copy/enqueue data here;
            // avoid doing UObject or gameplay work directly in this callback.
        },
        1024);

    if (!bOpened || !Capture->StartStream())
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Warning, TEXT("Could not start audio capture."));
        StopMicCapture();
    }
}

void AMyGameMode::StopMicCapture()
{
    if (Capture)
    {
        Capture->StopStream();
        Capture->CloseStream();
        Capture.Reset();
    }
}

void AMyGameMode::EndPlay(const EEndPlayReason::Type EndPlayReason)
{
    StopMicCapture();
    Super::EndPlay(EndPlayReason);
}
```

## Evaluation

Assistant B is better, although neither answer is reliably compile-ready.

B uses the more appropriate general approach for “AudioCapture, not AudioCaptureComponent”: managing a native capture-device interface rather than assuming a component is required. Its `FAudioCapture` / `IAudioCapture` direction is closer to the underlying capture API. However, its exact API calls and parameter setup are version-dependent and may be incorrect: in the current-style API, the callback is typically supplied to the stream-opening call, rather than set as `CaptureParams.Callback`. Its polling example is explicitly pseudocode, and the answer omits stopping and closing the stream when the GameMode ends.

A has more serious technical problems. The proposed `FSoundData` callback and `OnAudioCaptured.AddDynamic` binding do not match the usual native capture callback API; `AddDynamic` also requires a suitable reflected function/delegate signature. Calling `Destroy()` on a `UObject` is not the normal cleanup mechanism, and the claimed thread-safety behavior is not guaranteed merely by using a dynamic delegate. The suggested include and module setup also do not clearly match the native capture API.

Both responses miss an important architectural caveat: `AGameMode` exists on the server in a networked game, not on each client. Microphone capture should normally be initiated on the local client, even if GameMode coordinates related gameplay state. My example also notes that API names vary by UE version; neither assistant clearly handles that uncertainty.

My final verdict is: [[B>A]]

